{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --find normalization --lines 80\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:dmr --lines 20\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve unirank-code:HiFormer\npython3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-code:HiFormer --start-line 1 --lines 170'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 271,\n  \"next_line\": 351,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\\\_goods\\\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n292: Take ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\\n293: Only parameters of non-zero sparse features appearing in each mini-batch needs to be updated in the scenario of SGD based optimization methods without regularization.\\n294: However, when adding ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization it needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters for each mini-batch, which leads to extremely heavy computations and is unacceptable with parameters scaling up to hundreds of millions.\\n295: Figure 3. Control function of PReLU and Dice.\\n296: In this paper, we introduce an efficient mini-batch aware regularizer, which only calculates the L2-norm over the parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch and makes the computation possible.\\n297: In fact, it is the embedding dictionary that contributes most of the parameters for CTR networks and arises the difficulty of heavy computation.\\n298: Let 𝐖∈ℝD×K\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K} denote parameters of the whole embedding dictionary, with DD as the dimensionality of the embedding vector and KK as the dimensionality of feature space.\\n299: Expand the ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization on 𝐖\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}} over samples\\n300: (4)\\n301: L2​(𝐖)=‖𝐖‖22=∑j=1K‖𝒘j‖22=∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮∑j=1KI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})&=\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\\\end{split}\\n302: where 𝒘j∈ℝD\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D} is the jj-th embedding vector,\\n303: I⁡(𝒙j≠0)I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0) denotes if the instance 𝒙\\\\bm{x} has the feature id jj, and njn_{j} denotes the number of occurrence for feature id jj in all samples. Eq.(4) can be transformed into Eq.(5) in the mini-batch aware manner\\n304: (5)\\n305: L2​(𝐖)=∑j=1K∑m=1B∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬmI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\n306: where BB denotes the number of mini-batches, ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m} denotes the mm-th mini-batch.\\n307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\\n309: Then Eq.(5) can be approximated by\\n310: (6)\\n311: L2​(𝐖)≈∑j=1K∑m=1Bαm​jnj​‖𝒘j‖22.L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})\\\\approx\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}.\\n312: In this way, we derive an approximated mini-batch aware version of ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization.\\n313: For the mm-th mini-batch, the gradient w.r.t. the embedding weights of feature jj is\\n314: (7)\\n315: 𝒘j←𝒘j−η⁡[1|ℬm|​∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬm∂L⁡(p⁡(𝒙),y)∂𝒘j+λ​αm​jnj​𝒘j],\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\leftarrow\\\\bm{w}_{j}-\\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}{\\\\frac{\\\\partial L(p(\\\\bm{x}),y)}{\\\\partial\\\\bm{w}_{j}}+\\\\lambda\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\bm{w}_{j}}\\\\right],\\n316: in which only parameters of features appearing in mm-th mini-batch participate in the computation of regularization.\\n317: 5.2. Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n318: PReLU (He\\n319: et al., 2015) is a commonly used activation function\\n320: (8)\\n321: f⁡(s)={s if ​s>0α​s if ​s≤0.=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,f(s)=\\\\begin{cases}s&\\\\text{ if }s>0\\\\\\\\\\n322: \\\\alpha s&\\\\text{ if }s\\\\leq 0.\\\\end{cases}=\\\\penalty\\\\ p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\n323: where ss is one dimension of the input of activation function f⁡(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) and p⁡(s)=I⁡(s>0)p(s)=I(s>0) is an indicator function which controls f⁡(s)f(s) to switch between two channels of f⁡(s)=sf(s)=s and f⁡(s)=α​sf(s)=\\\\alpha s. α\\\\alpha in the second channel is a learning parameter.\\n324: Here we refer to p⁡(s)p(s) as the control function. The left part of Fig.3 plots the control function of PReLU.\\n325: PReLU takes a hard rectified point with value of 00, which may be not suitable when the inputs of each layer follow different distributions.\\n326: Take this into consideration, we design a novel data adaptive activation function named Dice,\\n327: (9)\\n328: f⁡(s)=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,p⁡(s)=11+e−s−E⁡[s]V​a​r​[s]+ϵf(s)=p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\\\ \\\\ p(s)=\\\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\\\frac{s-E[s]}{\\\\sqrt{Var[s]+\\\\epsilon}}}}\\n329: with the control function to be plotted in the right part of Fig.3.\\n330: In the training phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is the mean and variance of input in each mini-batch.\\n331: In the testing phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is calculated by moving averages E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] over data.\\n332: ϵ\\\\epsilon is a small constant which is set to be 10−810^{-8} in our practice.\\n333: Dice can be viewed as a generalization of PReLu.\\n334: The key idea of Dice is to adaptively adjust the rectified point according to distribution of input data, whose value is set to be the mean of input.\\n335: Besides, Dice controls smoothly to switch between the two channels.\\n336: When E⁡(s)=0E(s)=0 and V​a​r​[s]=0Var[s]=0, Dice degenerates into PReLU.\\n337: 6. Experiments\\n338: In this section, we present our experiments in detail, including datasets, evaluation metric, experimental setup, model comparison and the corresponding analysis.\\n339: Experiments on two public datasets with user behaviors as well as a dataset collected from the display advertising system in Alibaba demonstrate the effectiveness of proposed approach which outperforms state-of-the-art methods on the CTR prediction task.\\n340: Both the public datasets and experiment codes are made available1.\\n341: 6.1. Datasets and Experimental Setup\\n342: Amazon Dataset22\\n343: 2\\n344: http://jmcauley.ucsd.edu/data/amazon/.\\n345: Amazon Dataset contains product reviews and metadata from Amazon, which is used as benchmark dataset(He and McAuley, 2016; Mcauley\\n346: et al., ; Veit\\n347: et al., 2015). We conduct experiments on a subset named Electronics, which contains 192,403 users, 63,001 goods, 801 categories and 1,689,188 samples. User behaviors in this dataset are rich, with more than 5 reviews for each users and goods. Features include goods_id, cate_id, user reviewed goods_id_list and cate_id_list. Let all behaviors of a user be (b1,b2,…,bk,…,bnb_{1},b_{2},\\\\ldots,b_{k},\\\\ldots,b_{n}), the task is to predict the (k+1)-th reviewed goods by making use of the first k reviewed goods.\\n348: Training dataset is generated with k=1,2,…,n−2k=1,2,\\\\ldots,n-2 for each user. In the test set, we predict the last one given the first n−1n-1 reviewed goods.\\n349: For all models, we use SGD as the optimizer with exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 1 and decay rate is set to 0.1. The mini-batch size is set to be 3232.\\n350: MovieLens Dataset33\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:dmr\",\n  \"url\": \"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/5346\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"author README only, not the blocked publisher paper\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"total_lines\": 9,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 10,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: # DMR\\n2: Deep Match to Rank Model for Personalized Click-Through Rate Prediction\\n3: \\n4: This code for DMR is implemented based on the Alimama Dataset (https://tianchi.aliyun.com/dataset/dataDetail?dataId=56), references the codes of DIEN (https://github.com/mouna99/dien).\\n5: \\n6: \\\"alimama_sampled.txt\\\" is a sampled dataset for testing the code.\\n7: \\n8: Notice that, the user behavior data in this dataset contains only category, brand and behavior type, but no item.\\n9: \"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:HiFormer\",\n  \"title\": \"HiFormer benchmark implementation\",\n  \"kind\": \"benchmark_reimplementation\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/HiFormer.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"content_read\": true,\n  \"read_scope\": \"Representative forward paths and structural definitions; no end-to-end reproduction of this method.\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"locators\": [\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"HiFormer.forward\",\n      \"line\": 113,\n      \"end_line\": 145\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"HiFormerTokenizer.forward\",\n      \"line\": 156,\n      \"end_line\": 159\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"HiFormerBlocks.forward\",\n      \"line\": 197,\n      \"end_line\": 201\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"LowRankLinear.forward\",\n      \"line\": 210,\n      \"end_line\": 211\n    },\n    {\n      \"symbol\": \"HiformerAttentionLayer.forward\",\n      \"line\": 232,\n      \"end_line\": 239\n    }\n  ],\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"ff8b2e9cf9112ff50a807e1b85b8236ecaa699f842b93b6209630cace8b454a3\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"ff8b2e9cf9112ff50a807e1b85b8236ecaa699f842b93b6209630cace8b454a3\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://unirank-code:HiFormer\",\n  \"text_lines\": 239\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-code:HiFormer\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/salmon1802/UniRank/blob/90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d/model_zoo/HiFormer.py\",\n  \"version\": \"git:90bb99a60724e975ec565836266fd86dcad0039d\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Full pinned benchmark source for inspection; not proof of equivalence to the original author method.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"ff8b2e9cf9112ff50a807e1b85b8236ecaa699f842b93b6209630cace8b454a3\",\n  \"total_lines\": 239,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 161,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: # =========================================================================\\n2: # Copyright (C) 2026. UniRank Authors. All rights reserved.\\n3: # \\n4: # Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the \\\"License\\\");\\n5: # you may not use this file except in compliance with the License.\\n6: # You may obtain a copy of the License at\\n7: #\\n8: #     http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\\n9: #\\n10: # Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software\\n11: # distributed under the License is distributed on an \\\"AS IS\\\" BASIS,\\n12: # WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.\\n13: # See the License for the specific language governing permissions and\\n14: # limitations under the License.\\n15: # =========================================================================\\n16: \\n17: import torch\\n18: from torch import nn\\n19: from unirank.pytorch.models import MultiTaskModel\\n20: from unirank.pytorch.layers import FeatureEmbedding, MLP_Block, MaskedAveragePooling, PerTokenFeedForward, MultiHeadTargetAttention, ScaledDotProductAttention\\n21: from unirank.pytorch.torch_utils import get_activation\\n22: from unirank.utils import not_in_whitelist\\n23: from unirank.pytorch.layers.tokenization import FieldWiseTokenizer\\n24: \\n25: \\n26: class HiFormer(MultiTaskModel):\\n27:     def __init__(self,\\n28:                  feature_map,\\n29:                  model_id=\\\"HiFormer\\\",\\n30:                  task=[\\\"binary_classification\\\"],\\n31:                  gpu=-1,\\n32:                  tower_activations=\\\"ReLU\\\",\\n33:                  tower_hidden_units=[128, 64],\\n34:                  embedding_dim=10,\\n35:                  num_layers=3,\\n36:                  attention_dropout=0,\\n37:                  expansion_factor=4,\\n38:                  num_tasks=4,\\n39:                  token_dim=64,\\n40:                  attention_dim=None,\\n41:                  num_heads=2,\\n42:                  qkv_rank=256,\\n43:                  net_dropout=0,\\n44:                  accumulation_steps=1,\\n45:                  attention_activation_type=\\\"SoftMax\\\",\\n46:                  **kwargs):\\n47:         super(HiFormer, self).__init__(feature_map,\\n48:                                        model_id=model_id,\\n49:                                        gpu=gpu,\\n50:                                        **kwargs)\\n51:         self.num_tasks = num_tasks\\n52:         self.feature_map = feature_map\\n53:         self.embedding_dim = embedding_dim\\n54:         self.token_dim = token_dim\\n55:         self.accumulation_steps = accumulation_steps\\n56:         self.num_field = feature_map.get_num_fields()\\n57:         # Track item and non-item feature dimensions\\n58:         self.item_info_dim = 0\\n59:         self.non_item_dim = 0\\n60:         for feat, spec in self.feature_map.features.items():\\n61:             if feat in self.feature_map.labels:\\n62:                 continue\\n63:             if spec.get(\\\"type\\\") == \\\"meta\\\":\\n64:                 continue\\n65:             emb_dim = spec.get(\\\"embedding_dim\\\", embedding_dim)\\n66:             if spec.get(\\\"source\\\") in [\\\"item\\\", \\\"action\\\"]:\\n67:                 self.item_info_dim += emb_dim\\n68:                 self.num_field += 1\\n69:             else:\\n70:                 self.non_item_dim += emb_dim\\n71: \\n72:         self.embedding_layer = FeatureEmbedding(feature_map, embedding_dim)\\n73:         self.unified_tokenizer_layer = HiFormerTokenizer(\\n74:             input_dim=embedding_dim,\\n75:             token_dim=token_dim,\\n76:             num_tokens=self.num_field,\\n77:             num_tasks=num_tasks\\n78:         )\\n79: \\n80:         self.attention_layers = MultiHeadTargetAttention(\\n81:             input_dim=self.item_info_dim,\\n82:             attention_dim=token_dim if attention_dim is None else attention_dim,\\n83:             dropout_rate=attention_dropout,\\n84:             attention_activation_type=attention_activation_type,\\n85:         )\\n86: \\n87:         self.unified_interaction_layers = HiFormerBlocks(token_dim=token_dim,\\n88:                                        num_token=num_tasks + self.num_field,\\n89:                                        num_layers=num_layers,\\n90:                                        expand=expansion_factor,\\n91:                                        num_heads=num_heads,\\n92:                                        qkv_rank=qkv_rank,\\n93:                                        net_dropout=net_dropout)\\n94:         self.tower = nn.ModuleList([MLP_Block(input_dim=token_dim,\\n95:                                               output_dim=1,\\n96:                                               hidden_units=tower_hidden_units,\\n97:                                               hidden_activations=tower_activations,\\n98:                                               output_activation=None,\\n99:                                               dropout_rates=net_dropout)\\n100:                                     for _ in range(num_tasks)])\\n101:         if isinstance(task, list):\\n102:             assert len(task) == num_tasks, \\\"the number of tasks must equal the length of \\\\\\\"task\\\\\\\"\\\"\\n103:             self.output_activation = nn.ModuleList([self.get_output_activation(str(t)) for t in task])\\n104:         else:\\n105:             self.output_activation = nn.ModuleList(\\n106:                 [self.get_output_activation(task) for _ in range(num_tasks)]\\n107:             )\\n108: \\n109:         self.compile(kwargs.get(\\\"dense_optimizer\\\"), kwargs[\\\"loss\\\"], kwargs.get(\\\"dense_learning_rate\\\"))\\n110:         self.reset_parameters()\\n111:         self.model_to_device()\\n112: \\n113:     def forward(self, inputs):\\n114:         batch_dict, item_dict, mask = self.get_inputs(inputs)\\n115:         batch_size = mask.shape[0]\\n116:         item_seq_emb = self.embedding_layer(item_dict, flatten_emb=True)\\n117:         item_seq_emb = item_seq_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.item_info_dim)\\n118: \\n119:         target_emb = item_seq_emb[:, -1, :]      # B x item_info_dim\\n120:         sequence_emb = item_seq_emb[:, 0:-1, :]  # B x T x item_info_dim\\n121: \\n122:         seq_pooling_emb = self.attention_layers(target_emb, sequence_emb, mask)\\n123: \\n124:         # Other non-sequential features -> NS tokens\\n125:         user_context_emb = self.embedding_layer(batch_dict, flatten_emb=False)\\n126:         feature_embeddings = torch.cat([user_context_emb,\\n127:                                         target_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.embedding_dim),\\n128:                                         seq_pooling_emb.view(batch_size, -1, self.embedding_dim)], dim=1)\\n129:         unified_tokens = self.unified_tokenizer_layer(feature_embeddings)\\n130: \\n131:         # unified model\\n132:         unified_tokens = self.activation_checkpoint(\\n133:             self.unified_interaction_layers,\\n134:             unified_tokens\\n135:         )\\n136: \\n137:         # use task tokens for task-specific towers\\n138:         task_tokens = unified_tokens[:, self.num_field:, :]  # [B, num_tasks, token_dim]\\n139:         tower_output = [self.tower[i](task_tokens[:, i, :]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n140:         y_pred = [self.output_activation[i](tower_output[i]) for i in range(self.num_tasks)]\\n141:         return_dict = {}\\n142:         labels = self.feature_map.labels\\n143:         for i in range(self.num_tasks):\\n144:             return_dict[\\\"{}_pred\\\".format(labels[i])] = y_pred[i]\\n145:         return return_dict\\n146: \\n147: \\n148: class HiFormerTokenizer(nn.Module):\\n149:     def __init__(self, input_dim, token_dim, num_tokens, num_tasks):\\n150:         super(HiFormerTokenizer, self).__init__()\\n151:         self.task_tokens = nn.init.xavier_uniform_(\\n152:             nn.Parameter(torch.empty(1, num_tasks, token_dim))\\n153:         )\\n154:         self.token_mlps = FieldWiseTokenizer(input_dim, token_dim, num_tokens)\\n155: \\n156:     def forward(self, feature_embeddings):\\n157:         tokens = self.token_mlps(feature_embeddings)\\n158:         task_tokens = self.task_tokens.expand(tokens.size(0), -1, -1)\\n159:         return torch.cat([tokens, task_tokens], dim=1)\\n160: \"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"The earlier models already pass explicit history counts to the prediction layers. The proposed change will therefore give normalized content and count-scaled content separate tokens, with matched controls that duplicate either representation or fuse them before tokenization. This tests how the signals are organized; the connection to DIN’s interest-intensity idea is established, but novelty remains unverified.\n"}}
